\b0 The evaluation of the Smart Fashion Advisor system is based on standard classification metrics that measure the effectiveness of the skin tone detection module. These metrics provide insights into how accurately the system identifies different skin tone categories and how well it minimizes prediction errors.\par
True Positive (TP) represents the number of instances where the system correctly classifies the skin tone of the user. For example, if a user has a medium skin tone and the system correctly identifies it as medium, it is counted as a true positive. True Negative (TN) refers to cases where the system correctly identifies that a particular class does not belong to the input. False Positive (FP) occurs when the system incorrectly predicts a skin tone category, which may lead to unsuitable color recommendations. False Negative (FN) represents cases where the system fails to detect the correct skin tone, thereby affecting the overall recommendation quality.\par
These metrics are essential in evaluating the reliability of the classification component of the system. A higher number of true positives and true negatives indicates better performance, while lower values of false positives and false negatives reflect fewer errors. In the context of the Smart Fashion Advisor, accurate classification directly impacts the quality of outfit recommendations, making these metrics crucial for system evaluation. By analyzing these parameters, the system\rquote s effectiveness can be assessed, and necessary improvements can be identified to enhance accuracy and user satisfaction [4][8].\b\par
